Here's a React.js app that creates a retro terminal loading animation using Tailwind CSS to style the components:
## Step 1: Set up the React App and Install Tailwind CSS

First, create a new React app using Create React App:
```bash
npx create-react-app voyeur-ai-loading
cd voyeur-ai-loading
```

Next, install Tailwind CSS and dependencies:
```bash
npm install tailwindcss postcss autoprefixer -D
```

Create a Tailwind CSS configuration file:
```bash
npx tailwindcss init -p
```

## Step 2: Set up Tailwind CSS

Open the `tailwind.config.js` file and update the configuration:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  content: [
    "./src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}",
  ],
  theme: {
    extend: {},
  },
  plugins: [],
};
```

## Step 3: Create the Loading Animation Components

Let's create two functional components: `LoadingStage1` and `LoadingStage2`.

### LoadingStage1.js:
```jsx
import React from 'react';

const LoadingStage1 = () => {
  return (
    <div className="relative h-screen flex items-center justify-center bg-black">
      <div className="relative w-3/4 h-3/4 bg-terminal rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
        <div className="absolute w-full h-full bg-scanlines animate-scanlines"></div>
        <div className="relative px-6 py-8 text-green-300 text-center">
          <h1 className="text-2xl font-semibold">VOYEUR AI</h1>
          <p className="mt-2">Initializing AI System...</p>
          <p className="mt-1">Loading...</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default LoadingStage1;
```

### LoadingStage2.js:
```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

const LoadingStage2 = () => {
  const loadingBarRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const loadingBar = loadingBarRef.current;
    const width = 0;
    const increment = 5;
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      if (width >= 100) {
        clearInterval(id);
      } else {
        width += increment;
        loadingBar.style.width = `${width}%`;
      }
    }, 50);

    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div className="relative h-screen flex items-center justify-center bg-black">
      <div className="relative w-3/4 h-3/4 bg-terminal rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
        <div className="absolute w-full h-full bg-scanlines animate-scanlines"></div>
        <div className="relative px-6 py-8 text-green-300 text-center">
          <h1 className="text-2xl font-semibold">VOYEUR AI</h1>
          <p className="mt-2">ANALYZING IMAGES</p>
          <div className="mt-4">
            <div className="relative h-2 bg-green-400 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
              <div className="absolute bg-green-600 h-2 rounded-full" ref={loadingBarRef}></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default LoadingStage2;
```

## Step 4: Create the App Component

Now, create the main `App.js` component to render the loading animation:
```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import LoadingStage1 from './LoadingStage1';
import LoadingStage2 from './LoadingStage2';

const App = () => {
  const [loadingStage, setLoadingStage] = useState(1);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      setLoadingStage(2);
    }, 3000);

    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div className="flex h-screen w-screen">
      {loadingStage === 1 && <LoadingStage1 />}
      {loadingStage === 2 && <LoadingStage2 />}
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

